---
import BlogLayout from "../../../../layouts/BlogLayout.astro";
import ArticleImage from "../../../../components/ui/ArticleImage.astro";
import Breadcrumbs from "../../../../components/layout/Breadcrumbs.astro";
---

<BlogLayout
  title="How VA Disability Income Affects Your Mortgage Qualification | Cliffco Mortgage"
  description="VA disability compensation is tax-free and treated favorably in mortgage underwriting. Most veterans with a rating don't realize the full advantage it gives them. Here's what to know."
  type="article"
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={[
    { name: "Home", href: "/" },
    { name: "Blog", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/" },
    { name: "VA Disability Income and Mortgage Qualification", href: "/mortgage-guides/blog/va-disability-income-mortgage-qualification/" },
  ]} />

  <article class="container-prose pt-12 pb-16">

    <h1 style="font-size: var(--text-display-lg);">How VA Disability Income Affects Your Mortgage Qualification and What Most Lenders Won't Tell You</h1>

    <ArticleImage />

    <p class="mt-8">If you receive VA disability compensation and are planning to buy a home, your disability income affects your mortgage qualification in ways that work significantly in your favor, and most veterans with a rating don't know the full picture. This article explains exactly how disability compensation is treated in mortgage underwriting and what advantages it creates that other types of income don't.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Two Advantages Most Lenders Don't Proactively Explain</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">VA disability compensation creates two distinct advantages in mortgage qualification that stack on top of the standard VA loan benefits.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The first is income counting. VA disability compensation counts as qualifying income for a mortgage. It is stable, government-issued, and not subject to employment risk, all characteristics that underwriters view favorably. Unlike some income types that require two years of history to count, disability compensation is typically accepted immediately because it comes from a verified federal source with no expected end date.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The second is tax treatment. VA disability compensation is not subject to federal income tax. When a lender uses non-taxable income in a mortgage qualification calculation, they are generally permitted to gross it up, meaning they can increase the qualifying income figure to reflect the pre-tax equivalent. The gross-up percentage varies by lender and loan program but can meaningfully increase the qualifying income number used in your debt-to-income calculation.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">The Funding Fee Exemption Most Veterans Miss</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Here's the specific benefit that veterans with a disability rating most commonly miss. Veterans who have a service-connected disability rating of 10% or higher are completely exempt from the VA funding fee. That fee, which ranges depending on the loan scenario, represents a meaningful cost savings at closing. For veterans who are unsure whether their disability rating qualifies them for the exemption, a VA-experienced lender can verify their status before closing.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">The exemption applies to surviving spouses of veterans who died in service or from a service-connected disability as well, which is covered in more detail in the surviving spouse article in this series.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">How Disability Income Is Treated Across Different Loan Types</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Under VA loan guidelines, disability compensation counts as stable qualifying income and can be grossed up for its non-taxable status. This is the most favorable treatment available for this income type.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Under conventional loan guidelines, the treatment is similar in principle but lenders vary in how consistently they apply the gross-up. A lender who doesn't regularly work with veterans or disability income may not proactively gross it up, which means the veteran's qualifying income is understated relative to what it should be. Asking your lender directly how they treat VA disability income is worth doing before you accept a pre-approval number that may be lower than your actual qualification.</p>

    <p class="mt-4">Under FHA guidelines, disability income also counts and can be grossed up, though the specific documentation requirements differ from VA guidelines.</p>

    <h2 class="mt-10">What to Do, In Order</h2>

    <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal list-inside space-y-2">
      <li><strong>Tell your lender about your VA disability compensation at the start of the process.</strong> It should be included in your qualifying income from the beginning, not added later as an afterthought.</li>
      <li><strong>Ask your lender specifically whether they are grossing up your disability income.</strong> If they aren't, ask why. For most non-taxable income including VA disability compensation, a gross-up is standard practice and increases your qualifying income.</li>
      <li><strong>Confirm your funding fee exemption status before closing.</strong> If you have a service-connected disability rating of 10% or higher, you should not be paying a VA funding fee. Verify this with your lender before the closing disclosure is finalized.</li>
      <li><strong>Gather your VA award letter as part of your documentation package.</strong> This letter confirms the amount and permanence of your disability compensation and is what lenders use to verify the income.</li>
      <li><strong>If you're using a conventional loan, make sure the lender is treating your disability income correctly.</strong> The qualification advantage of grossing up non-taxable income applies regardless of loan type, but it requires a lender who knows to apply it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="mt-10">See What Your VA Loan Payment Would Look Like</h2>

    <p class="mt-4">Use the VA Loan Payment Estimator below to see your monthly payment and funding fee. If you have a service-connected disability rating of 10% or higher, select "Exempt" — you pay no funding fee, and that difference shows directly in your numbers.</p>

    <!-- Embedded lite VA Loan Payment Estimator -->
    <div class="mt-12 border border-[var(--color-accent)]/25 rounded-2xl overflow-hidden">
      <div class="bg-[var(--color-accent)]/8 px-6 py-5 border-b border-[var(--color-accent)]/15">
        <p class="eyebrow text-xs mb-1">Quick check</p>
        <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold text-[var(--color-ink)]">Estimate your VA loan payment.</h2>
        <p class="text-sm text-[var(--color-text-muted)] mt-1">Enter your purchase price, down payment, and VA use status to see your monthly payment and funding fee.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="p-6">
        <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Purchase price ($)</label>
            <input id="art-va-price" type="number" value="400000" min="50000" step="5000" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Down payment</label>
            <select id="art-va-down" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="0">0% (no down payment)</option>
              <option value="5">5%</option>
              <option value="10">10%</option>
            </select>
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">Interest rate (%)</label>
            <input id="art-va-rate" type="number" value="6.5" min="1" max="15" step="0.125" class="art-calc-input" />
          </div>
          <div>
            <label class="block text-sm font-medium text-[var(--color-ink)] mb-1">VA loan use</label>
            <select id="art-va-use" class="art-calc-input">
              <option value="first">First use</option>
              <option value="subsequent">Subsequent use</option>
              <option value="exempt">Exempt (disability / surviving spouse)</option>
            </select>
          </div>
        </div>
        <button id="art-va-btn" class="btn btn-primary mt-5">Estimate VA Payment</button>

        <div id="art-va-result" class="hidden mt-6">
          <div class="p-5 bg-[var(--color-mist-gray)] rounded-xl">
            <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Total Loan Amount</p>
                <p id="art-va-loan" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">VA Funding Fee</p>
                <p id="art-va-fee" class="text-2xl font-bold text-[var(--color-ink)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">Monthly P&amp;I</p>
                <p id="art-va-pi" class="text-3xl font-bold text-[var(--color-accent)] mt-1"></p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)] uppercase tracking-wide">vs. Conventional</p>
                <p id="art-va-conv" class="text-2xl font-bold mt-1"></p>
              </div>
            </div>
            <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">Estimate only. Does not include property taxes, homeowners insurance, or other costs. VA funding fee rates current as of 2025.</p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-4 p-5 bg-[var(--color-rich-black)] rounded-xl flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:items-center gap-4 justify-between">
            <div>
              <p class="text-white font-semibold">Ready to use your VA benefit?</p>
              <p class="text-white/70 text-sm mt-1">A Cliffco VA loan specialist can confirm your eligibility, COE status, and get you pre-approved.</p>
            </div>
            <a href="/get-started/" class="btn btn-primary !bg-white !text-[var(--color-rich-black)] shrink-0 whitespace-nowrap">Talk to a loan officer</a>
          </div>

          <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-center text-[var(--color-text-muted)]">
            Want to model other scenarios? <a href="/mortgage-guides/calculators/va-loan/" class="text-[var(--color-accent)] font-medium">View the full VA loan calculator →</a>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BlogLayout>

<style>
  .art-calc-input {
    width: 100%;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-soft-gray, #d1d5db);
    border-radius: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.75rem;
    font-size: 0.875rem;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: white;
    outline: none;
    transition: border-color 0.15s;
  }
  .art-calc-input:focus {
    border-color: var(--color-accent);
    box-shadow: 0 0 0 3px color-mix(in srgb, var(--color-accent) 15%, transparent);
  }
</style>

<script>
  const vaBtn = document.getElementById("art-va-btn")!;
  vaBtn.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const price = +(document.getElementById("art-va-price") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const downPct = +(document.getElementById("art-va-down") as HTMLSelectElement).value;
    const rate = +(document.getElementById("art-va-rate") as HTMLInputElement).value;
    const use = (document.getElementById("art-va-use") as HTMLSelectElement).value;

    const downAmt = price * downPct / 100;
    const baseLoan = price - downAmt;

    let feeRate = 0;
    if (use === "exempt") {
      feeRate = 0;
    } else if (use === "first") {
      feeRate = downPct >= 10 ? 0.014 : downPct >= 5 ? 0.015 : 0.023;
    } else {
      feeRate = downPct >= 10 ? 0.014 : downPct >= 5 ? 0.015 : 0.036;
    }

    const fundingFee = baseLoan * feeRate;
    const totalLoan = baseLoan + fundingFee;

    function monthlyPI(principal: number, annualRate: number, termYears: number): number {
      const r = annualRate / 100 / 12;
      const n = termYears * 12;
      if (r === 0) return principal / n;
      return principal * (r * Math.pow(1 + r, n)) / (Math.pow(1 + r, n) - 1);
    }

    const vaPI = monthlyPI(totalLoan, rate, 30);
    const convPI = monthlyPI(baseLoan, rate, 30);
    const savings = convPI - vaPI;
    const fmt = (v: number) => "$" + Math.round(v).toLocaleString("en-US");

    (document.getElementById("art-va-loan") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(totalLoan);
    (document.getElementById("art-va-fee") as HTMLElement).textContent =
      use === "exempt" ? "Exempt ($0)" : fmt(fundingFee);
    (document.getElementById("art-va-pi") as HTMLElement).textContent = fmt(vaPI);

    const vsEl = document.getElementById("art-va-conv") as HTMLElement;
    vsEl.textContent = savings >= 0
      ? fmt(savings) + " lower"
      : fmt(Math.abs(savings)) + " higher";
    vsEl.className = "text-2xl font-bold mt-1 " + (savings >= 0 ? "text-green-600" : "text-red-600");

    (document.getElementById("art-va-result") as HTMLElement).classList.remove("hidden");
  });
</script>
